Cysteine Sulfhydration
Sulfhydration (also referred to as persulfidation or sulfuration) is a posttranslational modification where a cysteine sulfhydryl group is modified to the persulfide form (RS-SH) (
Table 3
). This modification may arise through several mechanisms. First, a cysteine side chain may first undergo oxidation (e.g., sulfenylation or glutathionylation), followed by reaction with H
2
S or HS
–
to yield a persulfide bond (
Paul and Snyder, 2012
). Once formed, persulfides have been speculated to trans-
S
-sulfhydrate neighboring cysteine residues (
Zhang et al., 2017
) similar to transnitrosation (section “Cysteine
S
-Nitrosation”). A sulfhydrated cysteine is more reactive than the native thiol due to the lower p
K
a
value of a sulfhydrated cysteine (CysSSH p
K
a
= 4.3; CysSH p
K
a
= 8.3) (
Cuevasanta et al., 2015
;
Shi and Carroll, 2020
), which has downstream potential implications for protein structure, interacting proteins, and signaling pathways (
Paul and Snyder, 2012
).
